WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.390
- In 2017, hip-hop
officially surpassed rock

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:06.210
to become the most popular
genre in the United States,

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.520
and it's held the top spot ever since.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.320
Rappers are the new rock stars

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.940
and are taking the headlining spots

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.070
at some of the largest music festivals.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.070
And the sound is global
with chart-topping rap songs

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.170
in Korean, French and Spanish.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.770
- But hip-hop was born out of
counterculture and rebellion.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.980
Now that it's mainstream, can
artists still break the mold?

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.050
Today we're exploring alternative hip-hop,

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.820
artists that put originality first

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.130
to innovate on an
already innovative genre.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.350
- But what does alternative even mean?

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.870
Is it something you can actually classify?

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:38.970
And is it fair to put
artists in a category

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.710
just for being unique?

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.960
(smooth hip-hop music)

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.410
Later, Linda and I are gonna work together

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.900
to create our own
alternative hip-hop beat.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.820
But first, let's break down what we mean

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.920
when we say alternative music.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.710
- Alternative hip-hop is
sort of a loose category

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.870
and not everyone agrees on its definition,

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.790
but we are using it to describe artists

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.490
who stand out from their
peers and whose originality

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:07.560
has made them highly influential.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.060
Artists like Digable Planets,
MF Doom, or even Odd Future.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.730
- To get more context
surrounding alternative hip-hop,

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.800
I sat down with Dr. Taj Frazier

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.530
from the PBS show Hip-Hop
and the Metaverse.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:21.150
When you think of alternative hip-hop,

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.390
what characteristics do you
think make it alternative?

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:25.800
- When I think of alternative hip-hop,

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.920
I think number one, I
don't think hip-hop came up

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.650
or folks within hip-hop came
up with that terminology.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.570
The deep thing about hip-hop, I think,

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:38.550
is that it's very, very
expansive and plural, you know?

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.580
So you have different subcultures

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.190
under the banner of hip-hop.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.170
- When people think of alternative music,

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.570
they usually associate
it with rock sub-genres

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.630
like shoegaze, grunge or indie.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.160
And that's probably because the term

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.720
originated in rock music.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.860
The phrase alternative music
was popularized in 1979

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.160
by Terry Tolkin, a music
journalist who used alternative

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.290
to describe the underground
bands he covered.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.540
During the '80s, the
label became a catch-all

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.820
for any music that didn't
match the commercial sounds

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.470
of mainstream rock and pop.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.360
But there was also a
lot of experimentation

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.090
going on in hip-hop
during the '80s and '90s.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.580
This time is called the
golden age of hip-hop.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.180
It is known for its innovation
in production and sound.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.370
This time also saw the mainstream rise

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.370
of gangster rap from Los Angeles
with blockbuster releases

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.980
like "Straight Outta Compton" from NWA.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:32.400
And on the East Coast, hip-hop
was dominated by hardcore rap

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.210
with artists like Public
Enemy and The Notorious BIG.

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.160
But music that didn't fit into this mold

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.260
earned the moniker alternative.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.050
- I think oftentimes what
was given kind of the name

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.320
of alt hip-hop were communities in hip-hop

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.320
that did not fit or match
the kind of boundaries

00:02:53.914 --> 00:02:56.730
or to some degree, tropes of what people

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.200
imagine hip-hop to be.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:02.190
- If you ain't have a
gun or hat to the side,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:03.180
then you ain't hip-hop.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.430
With their debut album "Bizarre Ride II,"

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:09.090
The Pharcyde became an
alternative hip-hop cult classic.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:12.180
The lyrics showed a wackiness
and a lightheartedness

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.440
different from the gangster rap

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.450
popular in Los Angeles at the time.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:18.660
The production from J-Swift
contained layers and layers

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.090
of samples with some really deep cuts

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:23.820
like this sax line from
an Eddie Russ song.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:25.828
(saxophone music)

00:03:25.828 --> 00:03:29.365
♪ She keeps on passin' me by ♪

00:03:29.365 --> 00:03:32.580
(saxophone music)

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.890
- When asked why they
strayed from gangster rap,

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.507
Imani from The Pharcyde
told Acclaim magazine,

00:03:37.507 --> 00:03:40.177
"See, the whole thing
is we're not gangsters.

00:03:40.177 --> 00:03:42.037
"We wanted to be true to ourselves,

00:03:42.037 --> 00:03:43.837
"and we weren't about
to fake being gangsters

00:03:43.837 --> 00:03:45.270
"just to make rap records."

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:46.560
There were other artists labeled

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.570
as alternative hip-hop at this time

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.360
like Brand Nubian, De La Soul,

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.700
Digable Planets, and many more.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.430
- So that's how alternative
hip-hop got started,

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.950
forming around the same
time as alternative rock,

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.830
to describe rap music that
didn't fall under the umbrella

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.510
of what was popular at the time.

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.940
And because this description is so broad,

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.650
there are a lot of hip-hop sub-genres

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.113
that are used in correlation
with alternative,

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.900
where conscious hip-hop describes music

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.790
with a social activism focus,

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.010
underground hip-hop often refers to music

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.630
made by indie artists.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.540
Where anti rap focuses on
rap that's self-deprecating,

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:25.230
experimental hip-hop refers
to music that's more abstract.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.330
But all of these terms are just labels

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.760
that are used interchangeably
with alternative.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.260
And this is a perfect example

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:34.470
of how diving deep into sub-genres
can get really confusing.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.450
And because hip-hop is always evolving

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.760
and some artists are so influential,

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.350
what might be considered alternative

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:41.460
when it's first released,

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.500
ends up becoming the norm years later.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:45.120
Take the Fugees, for example,

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.730
who mix Afro-Cuban sounds
with reggae and soul

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:50.344
to create a truly unique
sound with the score.

00:04:50.344 --> 00:04:54.657
♪ Oh la la la la la la la la la la la ♪

00:04:54.657 --> 00:04:56.700
♪ Sweet thing yeah yeah ♪

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.260
- The score went on to win a Grammy

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.300
and go seven times platinum.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.520
And because it's so
cemented in pop culture

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.950
and has been so influential
to other artists,

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:05.970
the music doesn't sound

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:08.790
as uniquely alternative as it once was.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:12.427
In 1996, A Tribe Called Quest
released the jazz rap album

00:05:12.427 --> 00:05:15.060
"Beats, Rhymes and Life"
which ended up debuting

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.610
at the number one spot
on the Billboard 200.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:19.680
And this confuses things further

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.600
because with the successful
alternative groups

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.310
like Fugees and Tribe,

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:26.640
alternative hip-hop
suddenly became mainstream.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.350
- Okay, but that was the '90s.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.810
Who are the alternative
hip-hop artists of today?

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.050
First off, popular hip-hop
in the last 20 years

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:35.250
sounds incredibly different

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:37.260
from how it sounded during the golden age.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.900
And oftentimes, unconventional production

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:41.550
is embraced by the mainstream.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:44.130
Imagine a hip-hop fan from 1990

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.500
listening to "Lollipop" by Lil Wayne.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.626
♪ Shawty want a thug ♪

00:05:49.626 --> 00:05:51.120
♪ Bottles in the club ♪

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:54.210
- The scratchy auto-tune
singing and bubbly production

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.070
might make a fan from 1990

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.020
question if this is even hip-hop at all.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.550
But the track went on to win the Grammy

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.070
for Best Rap Song in 2009.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.470
Lil Wayne's song "Lollipop"
ushered in a wave

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.030
of auto-tune singing rappers

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.770
that went on to be chart toppers

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:11.580
like Lil Uzi Vert, Young
Thug and Coi Leray.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.680
So if that's popular rap today,

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:15.630
what is alternative hip-hop like?

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:17.550
Today's alternative hip-hop artists

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:19.230
are continuing to experiment

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:21.660
by combining genres from across the world

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:22.980
to create a sound distinct

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.720
from what's trending on streaming.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.610
Take Odd Future for example,

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:28.140
one of the most famous collectives

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:29.850
in alternative hip-hop today.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.790
When Tyler, the Creator
released "Igor" in 2019,

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:35.670
he departed from the horrorcore
rap the group was known for

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:39.060
and leaned into funk,
neo soul, and soft rock.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:40.620
Just listen to this interpolation

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:43.920
of Japanese soft rock
artists Tatsuro Yamashita

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.200
and his song "Gone, Gone/Thank You"

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.208
♪ Thank you for the love ♪

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:51.560
♪ Thank you for the joy ♪

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:53.921
♪ Thank you for your love ♪

00:06:53.921 --> 00:06:56.490
♪ Thank you for the heart ♪

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:58.380
- Another reason a modern artist

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.180
might earn the alternative moniker

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.510
is for experimenting with
conventional hip-hop aesthetics.

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:05.850
For example, take Tierra Whack's music

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.400
which has been described as
experimental by Pitchfork

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.977
for its use of vocal processing
and psychedelic melodies.

00:07:11.977 --> 00:07:13.761
♪ Heard no flow like this ♪

00:07:13.761 --> 00:07:15.839
♪ Make you say yo excuse me miss ♪

00:07:15.839 --> 00:07:18.270
♪ Go against me I'll beat like Swiss ♪

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:19.470
- To get a better understanding

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:22.770
of alternative hip-hop today,
I talked to Jean Deaux,

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:24.570
a rapper and singer who's known for mixing

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:26.580
a wide range of genres in her music.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:29.850
I've seen websites describe
your music as alternative R&amp;B.

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:31.920
What do you think of that description?

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:34.860
- I think alternative
is another way to say

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:39.360
out of the box or just non-traditional,

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:40.980
so I fully accept that label

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:43.360
because I know that I'm not making

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:47.760
the classic version of R&amp;B or hip-hop

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:48.900
that people are used to.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.326
So I fully embraced that all the way.

00:07:52.326 --> 00:07:54.357
♪ 'Cause I knew I could ♪

00:07:54.357 --> 00:07:56.310
♪ She say she need me
'cause she know it's good ♪

00:07:56.310 --> 00:08:01.310
I definitely took some
inspiration from Santigold.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:04.260
A lot of the music that
I listened to growing up

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:06.090
in high school like Lykke Li

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:09.663
and a little bit of Amy Winehouse.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.840
A lot of rap and hip-hop, you know,

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:14.403
obviously Missy Elliot.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.520
- One thing that Tierra Whack, Jean Deaux

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.650
and Tyler, the Creator all have in common

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:21.480
is that they all credit Missy Elliot

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:23.130
for being their inspiration.

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.687
In 2022, Tyler, the Creator tweeted,

00:08:25.687 --> 00:08:28.770
"To my younger fans, go
study all her albums."

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:31.620
Missy Elliot broke norms when
she entered the music world

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:33.630
by being her unapologetic self.

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:35.760
She was known for constantly experimenting

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:37.260
and evolving her sound.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:39.067
In 2022, she tweeted,

00:08:39.067 --> 00:08:42.067
"Sophomore albums are a very
pivotal time for an artist.

00:08:42.067 --> 00:08:43.957
"It's the best album to experiment on

00:08:43.957 --> 00:08:47.070
"because if you play
safe, you'll be boxed in."

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.230
In Missy's sophomore
albums "Da Real World,"

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:50.850
she switched it up by experimenting

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:53.370
with the grittier, retro-futuristic sound.

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:56.070
The album's aesthetics pictured
a surreal sci-fi future

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:57.780
that championed women's empowerment.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.430
Can you talk to me about Missy Elliot

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:02.730
and what she means as just an icon,

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:04.890
as an inspiration to you?

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:07.170
- Whoa, we could be here all day.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:12.170
She is rock and roll, she's
hip-hop, she is classic R&amp;B.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.910
Everything down to the videos,
to the visual artistry,

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.910
to her kind of like breaking the mold.

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:22.950
I think she just got to a place where

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:24.990
it was undeniable, her talent,

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:28.590
and she kind of made room for herself.

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:31.230
She's always inspiring me as a artist

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.840
to just push the boundary
a little bit more

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:35.790
and to have that courage

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.880
to go out on a limb and take a risk.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:40.830
- Missy Elliot wasn't alone.

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:42.420
The early 2000s were filled

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:45.210
with highly influential
alternative hip-hop artists

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:48.330
like Outkast, Doomtree and MF Doom.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.220
- Then there's the
cross-pollination that exists

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:52.800
between alternative rock and hip-hop.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:54.330
During the late 2000s,

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:56.670
indie rock saw a huge boom in popularity.

00:09:56.670 --> 00:10:00.570
In 2009, Jay-Z was praising
the indie band Grizzly Bear

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:02.160
and said, "What the indie rock movement

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:04.680
is doing right now is really inspiring."

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:07.320
Since then, many rappers have collaborated

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:08.820
with indie rock artists

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.160
like Kendrick Lamar with Tame Impala,

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:16.160
Kanye with Bony Iver, and Kid
Cuddy with MGMT and Ratatat.

00:10:16.641 --> 00:10:20.610
♪ I'm on the pursuit of
happiness and I know ♪

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.470
- [Linda] There's also
an entire wave of artists

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:24.270
who have been pushing past the limitations

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:26.190
of what hip-hop sounded like in the past.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:29.580
Artists like Death Grips,
Danny Brown and Clipping.

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:31.620
Often called experimental hip-hop,

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:33.420
Clipping has blended the hip-hop sound

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:36.282
with avant-garde genres
like industrial and noise.

00:10:36.282 --> 00:10:39.370
♪ Why you grab one right
by the dream space ♪

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:41.940
♪ Make one scream until she pray ♪

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:44.970
- So what exactly is alternative hip-hop?

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:46.770
Well, all of the artists that we mentioned

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:48.360
are so different from each other

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:51.600
that it's hard to call it a
sub-genre of hip-hop at all.

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:52.860
- And considering the success

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.560
that some of these artists
have seen, it's not fair to say

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:58.140
that it's only music
outside of the mainstream.

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:01.800
- Alt hip-hop often refers to
music that feels unexpected,

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.440
especially music that doesn't
fit into the common tropes

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.360
that the public has for hip-hop.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:09.960
- Lil Yachty's newest album is
the perfect example of this.

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:11.640
The mumble rapper completely departed

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:13.410
from what his fans knew him for

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:15.480
and created a psychedelic rock album

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:17.610
that ended up being praised by critics.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:18.873
♪ Up when it's cold ♪

00:11:18.873 --> 00:11:21.282
♪ You call me bestie ♪

00:11:21.282 --> 00:11:22.959
♪ Don't test me ♪

00:11:22.959 --> 00:11:24.090
♪ I'm too sexy ♪

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:25.830
- [Linda] Now that we've
learned a little bit more

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:27.750
about what alternative hip-hop is,

